/*
:name: wire_loop_local_decl
:type: error
:error: for-loop body may not drive wire 'w'
:help: drive the wire outside the loop
*/

// lhdtrack suggestions 2.3 / user ruling 2026-09-27 (ruling 5): a loop body may
// READ a `wire` (the loop is then unrolled), but it may not WRITE (drive) one.
// `wire w:U8 = a ^ i` is "declare and drive in one statement" (docs
// 04-variables.md), so declaring a wire INSIDE the body drives it once per
// iteration and stays an error, exactly like writing an enclosing wire
// (errors/wire_loop_write.prp, docs 05b-statements.md "Loops and `wire`").
//
// The body first READS the enclosing wire `back`. That read is legal after the
// ruling, so the diagnostic must name the DRIVE of `w` on its own line, not the
// read. Before the ruling, prp2lnast rejected the read instead:
//   for-loop body may not read or write wire 'back'   (on the `const b` line)
// The wording now that reads are legal:
//   message: for-loop body may not drive wire 'w'
//   hint:    drive the wire outside the loop; a loop body may only read a wire
// (a `while`/`loop` body says `loop body may not drive wire ...`, see
// errors/wire_while_loop_write.prp).
mod wire_loop_local_decl(a:U8) -> (z:U10@[0]) {
  wire back:U8 = nil
  mut acc:U10 = 0
  for i in 0..<4 {
    const b = back          // a READ of an enclosing wire: legal
    wire w:U8 = a ^ i  // locate_error_here
    acc = acc + (w & b)
  }
  back = a
  z = acc
}
